WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.800
Govern is central in CSF 2.0 because it describes how cybersecurity risk management is

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:06.900
directed,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.520
prioritized, and overseen.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.380
It connects cybersecurity to enterprise risk.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.520
That connection matters because cybersecurity is not only a technical activity.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.260
It affects operations, finance, legal obligations, customers, safety, suppliers,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.880
reputation, and mission delivery.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.500
Govern helps an organization decide why security matters, who owns decisions,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.560
and how risk is communicated.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:37.620
Govern includes strategy, policy, roles, responsibilities, authority, oversight,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.680
organizational context, and risk management expectations.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.180
Good governance does not mean paperwork for its own sake.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.720
It means the right people understand their role, the right owners can make decisions,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.260
and teams know how priorities are set.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.800
Without governance, technical teams may collect evidence and build controls,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.660
but still lack a clear path for exceptions, tradeoffs, funding, and accountability.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.040
Supply chain risk is also part of CSF 2.0 governance.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:11.140
Organizations rely on vendors, cloud providers, software suppliers, service providers,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.600
contractors, and partners.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.620
A supplier can affect cybersecurity risk even when the system is not directly owned by the

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.960
organization.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.460
Governance should clarify who evaluates supplier risk, how requirements are communicated,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.200
what evidence is needed, and how supplier issues are tracked.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.660
Govern makes the other Functions actionable.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:39.400
Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover all require priorities, owners, policies,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.900
and oversight.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.520
When governance is weak, the framework can become a list of disconnected tasks.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.340
When governance is clear,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.920
CSF 2.0 becomes a decision system for setting direction and managing risk responsibly.
